# JavaScript Expressions in JSX

The JSX so far has been **static**: whatever you typed is what appears. Interfaces usually need to reflect something that changes, which means getting JavaScript values into the markup.

In JSX, **curly braces** do that. Whatever is inside them is evaluated, and the resulting value is inserted into the output.

```{react autorun}
function Greeting() {
  const name = 'Bob';

  return (
    <p>
      Hello, {name}. One plus one equals {1 + 1}.
    </p>
  );
}

renderReact(<Greeting />);
```

Two sets of braces, two values inserted. Change `Bob` to your own name and run it again.

## Expressions, Not Statements

The word **expression** is doing real work in that sentence, and it is the thing to take away from this lesson.

An **expression** produces a value: `name`, `1 + 1`, `score > 10`, `name.toUpperCase()`, `items.length`.

A **statement** performs an action and produces no value: `if`, `for`, `while`, `return`, a variable declaration.

**Only expressions go in the braces.** This is a hard rule, and it follows from what braces mean: React is going to take the value and put it in the output, so there has to be a value.

```jsx
{/* Fine: each of these produces a value */}
<p>{name}</p>
<p>{name.toUpperCase()}</p>
<p>{score + bonus}</p>
<p>{items.length}</p>

{/* Not allowed: an if statement produces no value */}
<p>{if (isReady) { name }}</p>
```

That last line is a syntax error, and it is the mistake everyone makes at least once. Conditionals in JSX are still possible, using expressions that do the same job. That is the subject of a later lesson.

## Any Expression, Not Only Variables

The braces do not hold a special mini-language. Any JavaScript that produces a value is fair game, including method calls and arithmetic:

```{react autorun}
function ProductRow() {
  const product = 'wireless mouse';
  const price = 24.5;
  const quantity = 3;

  return (
    <ul>
      <li>{product}</li>
      <li>{product.toUpperCase()}</li>
      <li>{price * quantity}</li>
      <li>{'Item: ' + product}</li>
      <li>{product.length} characters in the name</li>
    </ul>
  );
}

renderReact(<ProductRow />);
```

Every one of those is an ordinary JavaScript expression that happens to be written inside markup. Try adding a line that shows the price rounded with `price.toFixed(2)`.

## Where Braces Can Go

Braces work in two places: as **content** between tags, and as an **attribute value**.

```{react autorun}
function Avatar() {
  const user = 'ada';
  const size = 60;

  return (
    <div>
      <img
        src={'https://example.com/' + user + '.png'}
        alt={'Profile picture for ' + user}
        width={size}
        height={size}
      />
      <p>Showing {user} at {size} pixels</p>
    </div>
  );
}

renderReact(<Avatar />);
```

Note `width={size}` rather than `width="size"`. With quotes you get the literal string `size`; with braces you get the value of the variable. This is a common early mistake and it is worth trying: change one of them to quotes and see what the attribute becomes.

## Exercises

1. Use the `name` variable inside the heading so the page reads `Hello, Grace`.

   ```{react}
   function Greeting() {
     const name = 'Grace';

     return <h1>Hello, name</h1>;
   }

   renderReact(<Greeting />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function Greeting() {
     const name = 'Grace';

     return <h1>Hello, {name}</h1>;
   }

   renderReact(<Greeting />);
   ```
   ```+test
   // React commits its render asynchronously, so the assertions below run
   // on the next tick. Asserting immediately would find an empty container.
   setTimeout(function () {
     assert('h1').should('have.text', 'Hello, Grace').run('Render `Hello, Grace` by putting the `name` variable in curly braces', 'Rendered the name');
   }, 50);
   ```

2. Show the total. Display the product name in uppercase, and the price multiplied by the quantity.

   ```{react}
   function Receipt() {
     const item = 'notebook';
     const price = 4;
     const quantity = 3;

     return (
       <div>
         <h2>{/* TODO: the item name, in uppercase */}</h2>
         <p>Total: {/* TODO: price times quantity */}</p>
       </div>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<Receipt />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function Receipt() {
     const item = 'notebook';
     const price = 4;
     const quantity = 3;

     return (
       <div>
         <h2>{item.toUpperCase()}</h2>
         <p>Total: {price * quantity}</p>
       </div>
     );
   }

   renderReact(<Receipt />);
   ```
   ```+test
   setTimeout(function () {
     assert('h2').should('have.text', 'NOTEBOOK').run('Show the item name in uppercase. Any expression works inside the braces, including `item.toUpperCase()`', 'Showed the uppercase name');
     assert('p').should('have.text', 'Total: 12').run('Show the price times the quantity, which is `12`', 'Showed the total');
   }, 50);
   ```

3. Set the image's `width` attribute from the `size` variable rather than from a literal string.

   ```{react}
   function Thumbnail() {
     const size = 80;

     return <img src="cat.png" alt="A cat" width="size" />;
   }

   renderReact(<Thumbnail />);
   ```
   ```+solution
   function Thumbnail() {
     const size = 80;

     return <img src="cat.png" alt="A cat" width={size} />;
   }

   renderReact(<Thumbnail />);
   ```
   ```+test
   setTimeout(function () {
     assert('img').should('have.attribute', 'width', '80').run('Use `width={size}` with braces. Quotes would give the literal text `size` instead of the value', 'Set the width from the variable');
   }, 50);
   ```

4. What can go inside JSX curly braces?

   ```{mcq}
   Which of these is allowed inside `{ }` in JSX?
   correct: Correct! Braces hold expressions, meaning code that produces a value. An `if` statement performs an action and produces nothing, so there would be nothing for React to insert.
   wrong: Ask of each option: does this produce a value that could be inserted into the output? Look back at "Expressions, Not Statements".
   [x] `score + 1`
   [ ] `if (ready) { name }`
   [ ] `for (const item of items) { }`
   [ ] `const total = 5;`
   ```

5. Why is a statement not allowed there?

   ```{mcq}
   What is the reason for the expressions-only rule?
   correct: Correct! React takes whatever the braces evaluate to and puts it in the output, so there has to be a value. A statement produces none.
   wrong: Think about what React does with the contents of the braces once it has evaluated them.
   [x] React inserts the resulting value into the output, and a statement produces no value
   [ ] Statements are too slow for React to run during a render
   [ ] Babel cannot parse statements at all
   [ ] Statements are allowed, but only one per component
   ```

